## 1. Proposed Architecture

`Telegram OS` employs a Modular Monolith architecture bound to a single authoritative PostgreSQL database and a shared Redis caching/event bus layer. This design eliminates split-brain data state across the Telegram Bot, Telegram Mini App (TMA), and Web Admin Dashboard while remaining straightforward to deploy and maintain.

```
                  +-------------------------------------------------------+
                  |                     CLIENT LAYER                      |
                  +-------------------+-------------------+---------------+
                                      |                   |
               Telegram App           |     Web App       |  Telegram App
             +---------------+        |  +-------------+  | +-----------+
             | Telegram Bot  |        |  | Admin Panel |  | | Mini App  |
             | (Bot API 7+)  |        |  | (React SPA) |  | | (TMA SDK) |
             +-------+-------+        |  +------+------+  | +-----+-----+
                     |                |         |         |       |
                     | Webhook        | HTTPS   | REST    | HTTPS | REST / initData
                     v                v         v         v       v
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                                  EDGE & GATEWAY                                   |
|  Nginx Reverse Proxy: SSL Termination, WAF, Rate Limiting (Token Bucket)         |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
                                      |
                                      v
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                              UNIFIED BACKEND ENGINE                               |
|                                                                                   |
|  +------------------+  +------------------+  +------------------+                 |
|  |   Auth Module    |  |   User Module    |  | Subscription Mod |                 |
|  | (HMAC validation)|  |  (RBAC/Profile)  |  |  (State Engine)  |                 |
|  +------------------+  +------------------+  +------------------+                 |
|  +------------------+  +------------------+  +------------------+                 |
|  | Payment Gateway  |  | Content Service  |  | Notification Engine|               |
|  | (Idempotency)    |  |  (Access Matrix) |  |  (Async Worker)  |                 |
|  +------------------+  +------------------+  +------------------+                 |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
                               |                  |
           SQLAlchemy 2.0      |                  | Async Redis Client
           Async Engine        v                  v
+--------------------------------------+  +-----------------------------------------+
|        PRIMARY DATABASE              |  |         CACHE & BROKER                  |
|  PostgreSQL 16 Engine                |  |  Redis 7 Cluster                        |
|  - Relational Schema & Foreign Keys  |  |  - Session Store                        |
|  - Transactional Isolation (Read Comm)| |  - Task Queue (ARQ / SAQ)              |
|  - Indexing & Check Constraints      |  |  - Distributed Locks & Rate Limits    |
+--------------------------------------+  +-----------------------------------------+

```

### Core Architecture Flow

1. **Single Source of Truth:** All user actions—whether initiated via inline bot commands, TMA interfaces, or Admin Panel configurations—transit through a unified FastAPI application domain service layer.
2. **Synchronous REST & Async Workflows:** Real-time state queries (e.g., balance check, user verification) run synchronously. Long-running tasks (e.g., broadcast notifications, payment reconciliation) are pushed to the Redis task queue for async processing.
3. **Stateless Service Execution:** App instances carry no in-memory state, allowing horizontal scaling behind the Nginx load balancer when traffic spikes occur.

